      "The tithi is rikta in the classical scheme, so ordinary almanacs advise against weddings or travel, but the shastras treat devotion to Ganesha as exempt from the category. The reasoning is that he is the remover of obstacles, so honouring him on an empty tithi fills it. Modaks, durva grass and red flowers are the traditional offerings, and some devotees count out twenty-one pieces of each.",
      "One curious custom attaches to the Bhadrapada occasion: people avoid looking at the moon that night, citing an old story in which seeing it led to a false accusation. Those who do see it are told to recite a particular verse. In Magha the bright Chaturthi is called Tilkund and marked with sesame. Practices differ by region, so follow the guidance of your own family or temple.",
