      "Like its bright-half twin it falls under rikta, but a Sankashti fast is not affected by that label. A devotee typically abstains until the moon is seen, offers water to it, worships Ganesha and breaks the fast with a light meal. When the day falls on a Tuesday it is called Angarki Sankashti and is regarded by many as especially potent, a belief rooted in popular tradition.",
      "The Magha occurrence is known in north India as Sakat Chauth or Tilkund, when mothers fast for their children, and sesame and jaggery sweets are central. Karva Chauth, the fast kept by married women for their husbands, also falls on a Krishna Chaturthi in Kartik. These festivals show how the same tithi has produced a variety of customs. Details depend on region, so check your local calendar.",
