      "Shukla Trayodashi is the thirteenth day of the waxing moon, one night short of the full-moon eve. It is the bright-half Pradosh day, when the vow of twilight worship of Shiva is kept. The moon is almost full and visible at dusk, so the devotional scene is an evening one: lamps, bilva leaves, bells and the sound of Om Namah Shivaya.",
      "It is a jaya tithi, a victory day, and its lord is Shiva in his Pradosh aspect. A traditional account says the gods and demons once churned the ocean, and when poison emerged, Shiva swallowed it to save the worlds. Popular belief places that event in this twilight, which is why abhishekam of the linga with milk, water, honey and curd is offered then. This is a story from the Puranas, retold in many forms.",
      "The weekday adds a layer of nuance: Monday occurrences are known as Soma Pradosh and are widely prized, while Tuesday and Saturday ones also have names. Vrishabha Pradosh in Vaishakha is another named occasion. The fast is kept all day and broken only after the evening worship, but those who cannot fast fully can still attend the evening rite. As always, the timing of the twilight window varies by location, so consult a local almanac.",
